msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:307
msgid "Current limitation:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:307
msgid "This plugin currently supports single-site WordPress installations only. Multisite and network activation are not yet supported."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:308
msgid "Database:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:308
msgid "Use MySQL 8.0 or newer or MariaDB 10.11 or newer with InnoDB available. MySQL 8.4 LTS is recommended for production. SQLite, PostgreSQL and unverified database-translation drop-ins are not supported."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:309
msgid "If an active site moves outside that database envelope, Booster leaves its stored data unchanged and pauses package storage, Transporter and deployments. Restore a supported database, then use read-only Troubleshooting to confirm compatibility. For planned moves, keep the normal site backup and a current Transporter Blueprint; Booster does not convert custom tables between database engines."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:310
msgid "PHP:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:310
msgid "PHP 8.2 or newer is required, 8.4 LTS or newer recommended."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:319
msgid "Move managed packages without copying a development checkout"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:320
msgid "A Transporter Blueprint is a ZIP file that records the Booster-managed packages you select on this site and where their repos live. It is not a copy of the plugin or theme files installed here. The site that opens the Blueprint downloads its files from the committed repository, so local uncommitted changes, and local-only"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:320
msgid "folders, and other ignored and uncommitted dependencies are never included."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:321
msgid "Before a database or environment move"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:323
msgid "Export a current Transporter Blueprint containing every managed package."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:324
msgid "Optionally include the selected packages’ file-stored repository credentials in the password-protected archive."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:325
msgid "Preview the ZIP successfully and retain a copy off-site."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:326
msgid "Keep the normal database and filesystem backup."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:328
msgid "Complete database copies inside the supported MySQL or MariaDB and InnoDB envelope are best effort. A Transporter Blueprint is the supported reconstruction route when the copied Booster tables cannot be trusted, but the target database must still satisfy Booster’s requirements. Booster does not convert custom tables between database engines."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:329
msgid "Deployment attempts and delivery-replay history, webhook secrets and hooks registered with the Git provider, constants, locks and worker state, and source deployment policy remain target-local. Every installed or adopted package starts with deployment Disabled, so an administrator must deliberately choose a new target policy."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:330
msgid "Transporter workflow:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:330
msgid "Select packages for a Transporter Blueprint on this site, open it on the other site, review what would change for each package, then apply only the selected changes. Excluded and unchecked packages remain untouched. Reviewing a Transporter Blueprint never installs anything, changes package settings, or stores the uploaded file — nothing happens until you apply it."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:331
msgid "Open Transporter"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:333
msgid "Step-by-step: moving a package to another site"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:335
msgid "Commit and push the exact branch, tag or commit you want the other site to install. A Transporter Blueprint never includes uncommitted local changes."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:336
msgid "If you are actively editing a package’s files on this site, set it to Disabled first so the Transporter Blueprint does not propose replacing files you are working on. Use Manual only when you want administrators on the other site to be able to update it through Booster."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:337
msgid "Choose which managed plugins and themes to include, then create a Transporter Blueprint. Credential transfer applies only to those selected packages and only when their credential is stored as a file rather than, for example, a constant. A package-only ZIP is unencrypted; including credentials requires a password and AES-256 encryption."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:338
msgid "On the other site, upload the ZIP, enter its password if you set one, then select Review Transporter Blueprint. Booster shows which packages it can install or adopt and which are already managed, protected, or blocked. An already-managed row can import its verified Blueprint credential without changing package settings; select only the actions you want Booster to apply."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:339
msgid "Apply only the changes you choose. Booster re-reads the ZIP and re-checks each package and repository right before applying it, so if something changed since your review, that one change is skipped safely and the others continue."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:340
msgid "On the other site, confirm repository access works and the package behaves as expected. Set up Push-to-Deploy separately, and only once that site is ready to receive automatic deployments."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:350
msgid "Local development"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:351
msgid "WordPress local or development configuration and localhost addresses provide development context. Debug mode or a nonstandard port alone does not establish that a site is local. These signals do not establish whether incoming webhooks can reach it."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:352
msgid "Booster can still download and replace managed plugins and themes on a local site. A local site running an official Booster release may also receive updates for Booster itself. Protection of Booster’s own source checkout is a separate policy; review its effective update status in Settings or Troubleshooting. Dismissing local-development guidance changes neither that protection nor any package’s Updates setting."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:353
msgid "Deployment modes protect files you are editing"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:355
msgid "blocks Booster branch reinstalls and push deployments, and suppresses managed release update offers. Use this for a package whose files on disk you are currently editing."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:356
msgid "allows an administrator-triggered branch reinstall but blocks Push-to-Deploy. For release-managed packages, it preserves WordPress’s existing automatic-update decision; it does not guarantee that automatic updates are disabled."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:357
msgid "allows authenticated matching branch pushes to deploy, or enables WordPress automatic updates for validated published releases, according to the package’s source."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:359
msgid "Booster never changes a package’s mode on its own. It may show a reminder when WordPress reports a local or development environment, plugin or theme development mode is active, WP_DEBUG is enabled, the site uses localhost or a loopback address, or its URL has a nonstandard port. These are safety hints only: Booster will not switch a package to Disabled or Manual for you. Choose each package’s mode explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:360
msgid "These settings govern Booster-managed replacement paths; they do not lock files against other tools. Manual and Automatic can replace local edits. Choose Disabled for each package you are editing."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:361
msgid "Incoming branch-deployment webhooks are generally unavailable on local or private domains. A public tunnel can make a local site reachable when the actual configured callback meets the existing public HTTPS requirements. URL acceptance is not a reachability test or proof of authenticated delivery."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:361
#: views/packages/fields/deployment-policy.php:67
msgid "Read the Push-to-Deploy guide."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:363
msgid "What a Transporter Blueprint includes"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:364
msgid "A Transporter Blueprint records only the Booster-managed plugins and themes selected on the source site. It can optionally include file-stored repository credentials used by those selected packages inside a password-protected ZIP. The ZIP password is only used for that one request and is never saved anywhere. When a package is installed or adopted from a Transporter Blueprint, it starts with deployment Disabled."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:365
msgid "A Transporter Blueprint does not include WordPress content, uploads, the database, the package files themselves, constants, webhook secrets, hooks registered with the Git provider, deployment attempts or delivery-replay history, locks or worker state, or source deployment policy. Push-to-Deploy is always configured separately on each site."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:367
msgid "Prepare the branch you are deploying"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:368
msgid "Booster installs exactly what is committed to the repository ref you select. It does not remove files, run build tools or install dependencies on the target site."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:370
msgid "You, or the package’s maintainer, are responsible for what the repository’s ignore, build and release process leaves in that ref."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:371
msgid "Include compiled or built assets and anything the package needs at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:372
msgid "is normally only needed to build the package, so it should usually be excluded from the ref."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:373
msgid "Include the Composer"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:373
msgid "directory if the package needs it at runtime and does not install it itself."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:383
msgid "Where credentials are stored"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:384
msgid "Prerelease restart required: before installing this version on an installation using secrets.json, export any configuration and repository credentials you want to keep, remove the old Booster stack and verify its owned state has been cleared. Provision fresh storage, then review a Transporter import or re-adopt existing packages. Old files are never discovered, renamed or deleted automatically. Transporter does not restore webhook secrets or remote hooks."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:385
msgid "Booster stores repository credentials and webhook secrets as authenticated ciphertext in a private JSON file outside the WordPress and plugin directories. Conventional single-site POSIX installations can use the protected Settings storage section’s automatic location. Containers and uncommon layouts should define"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:385
msgid "as a private directory outside the public web root on durable local storage. The configured value must evaluate to an absolute canonical path; in wp-config.php, an expression such as"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:385
msgid "provides a safe relative layout anchored to that file. The directory must be owned by PHP, readable and writable by PHP, and mode 0700; Booster manages ran-booster-store.json inside it. Raw unanchored relative strings are rejected because web, cron and CLI processes may have different working directories. Booster never displays a saved secret back to you once it is stored."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:386
msgid "The file and lock are owner-only. Its independent encryption key is held in the non-autoloaded"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:386
msgid "WordPress option, so neither the filesystem nor database alone contains usable credentials. Restore the matching encrypted file and database key from the same backup, and protect that backup as secret material."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:388
msgid "Each administrator can dismiss their own credential expiry reminders. A five-day snooze is available only when every credential in the reminder expires more than five days from now. A new or replaced expiry will warn you again. Acknowledging a deployment failure banner does not resolve the incident."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:390
#: views/settings-storage.php:124
msgid "Use a different storage location"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:391
msgid "Choose a durable directory outside the public web root that is owned by the same PHP process user used by WordPress web, cron and CLI requests. The directory must use mode 0700. Do not add group access as a workaround: Booster deliberately requires owner-only access. Define the preferred directory constant in wp-config.php before WordPress loads plugins; Booster creates and manages ran-booster-store.json and ran-booster-store.json.lock inside it with mode 0600:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:392
msgid "Code example: storage directory constant"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:393
msgid "In wp-config.php, __DIR__ is the directory containing wp-config.php and dirname( __DIR__ ) is its parent. If wp-config.php is in public_html, do not append public_html again: choose a sibling directory outside it. Every existing parent directory must let the PHP process traverse it; on shared hosting that may require a host-managed execute permission or ACL on an ancestor. The final Booster storage directory must still be owned by PHP and mode 0700."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:394
msgid "To use a hosting environment variable, bridge it to the PHP constant in wp-config.php. An environment variable by itself is not read by Booster:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:395
msgid "Code example: storage directory from an environment variable"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:399
msgid "If credentials already exist, move or restore ran-booster-store.json, ran-booster-store.json.lock and the matching database key together. Do not create an empty secrets file, copy only the encrypted file, or reset only the database key. If either the file or database key is permanently unavailable, the protected Settings storage section can offer an explicit typed reset after Booster verifies the remaining filesystem material and private path; this discards unrecoverable credentials and webhook secrets before fresh storage can be initialized."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:400
msgid "Moving credentials between sites"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:401
msgid "A plugin ZIP export or a database-only site migration cannot recreate the encrypted store. Transporter, described above, is the supported way to move selected repository credentials: it protects them inside the Transporter Blueprint ZIP, confirms repository identity and access on the target, and re-encrypts them with the target site’s key. Installing or adopting a package associates the imported credential with that package. Credential-only recovery for an already-managed package imports the credential under a new target-local ID but deliberately leaves the package’s saved credential selection unchanged; assign the recovered credential through the package settings if needed. Constants, webhook secrets and credentials already saved on the target are never exported. When Transporter is not the right fit, add credentials directly through the Git provider settings screens on the target site, or use the supported deployment constants instead."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:402
msgid "Do not overwrite an existing target site’s credentials file. If you ever need to restore the whole file as a recovery step, keep a copy of the target’s existing file first, and treat that restore as something only an approved recovery process should do."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:403
msgid "Keep the encrypted file and key protected, and exclude the file from version control and from any release archive."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:405
msgid "What happens on uninstall"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:406
msgid "Deactivating Booster keeps all credentials, package settings and deployment history in place. Deleting Booster through WordPress is the permanent local cleanup action: it removes both Booster custom tables, the encrypted credentials file and its separate database key, other Booster options, user notices, scheduled work, locks and temporary Logging capture. If Booster cannot verify or remove one of those artifacts safely, WordPress keeps the plugin files so an administrator can correct the problem and retry."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:407
msgid "Before deleting Booster, export a password-protected Transporter Blueprint with the selected packages and any file-stored repository credentials they use that you may need. A Transporter Blueprint does not include webhook secrets, webhook registrations on the Git provider, deployment history or constants, and every restored package starts with deployment Disabled."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:408
msgid "Uninstall cannot revoke Git provider credentials or remove remote webhooks. Revoke credentials with each Git provider and remove remote hooks before uninstalling. Booster removes only the exact wp-config.php storage definition it created; any manually authored storage definition remains under the site operator’s control."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:417
msgid "Choose a Git provider, then search for a repository or enter its account and name manually. Booster resolves repository visibility, stable identity, and the default branch before installation."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:420
#: views/onboarding.php:208
#: views/overview-dashboard.php:84
#: views/provider.php:773
msgid "Install a theme"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:421
msgid "Manage deployed plugins"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:422
msgid "Manage deployed themes"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:424
msgid "A saved credential is required only when the selected repository is private or otherwise inaccessible anonymously. Keep the configured branch aligned with the branch you intend to deploy."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:426
msgid "Large repository archives"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: registered repository provider labels.
#: views/documentation.php:430
#, php-format
msgid "%s provide Booster with a ZIP of the whole repository. Choosing a plugin or theme subdirectory does not make that download smaller. Keep development-only trees such as committed node_modules, caches, test artifacts and unused build output out of the deployed repository ref."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: compressed ZIP limit in MiB, 2: expanded ZIP limit in MiB.
#: views/documentation.php:438
#, php-format
msgid "This site currently allows repository ZIPs up to %1$d MiB compressed and %2$d MiB expanded."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:446
msgid "Archive deployments are currently blocked:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:446
msgid "RAN_BOOSTER_MAX_DOWNLOAD_BYTES must be an integer from 1 MiB through 536,870,911 bytes (just under 512 MiB)."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:448
msgid "Adjust the compressed limit in Booster Settings. A target-local constant overrides and locks that preference:"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:449
msgid "Code example: download limit constant"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:450
msgid "The value may range from 1 MiB through 536,870,911 bytes (just under 512 MiB). Booster derives a separate expanded-content limit four times as large and still applies entry-count, path, identity and free-space checks. It does not set installed size or History retention. It affects branch-source manual installs and updates, webhook updates, and package installation from a Transporter Blueprint. GitHub release inspection, installation and managed updates also use the compressed limit; the release updater has separate expansion safeguards. Extension downloads and Blueprint file imports have independent bounds."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:459
msgid "Push-to-Deploy is optional and configured separately on every target site. Use the matching Git provider tab as the source of truth for the secret scope, payload URL, content type and event. Follow that Git provider’s webhook instructions, test delivery, and only then deliberately set the installed package to Automatic. The Webhook receiver tab shows one shared receiver: Automated setup opens a repository’s webhook controls, and Manual setup lists the payload URL, content type, event and detailed instructions."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:460
msgid "The Git provider screen reports site readiness, stable repository identity and whether a local signing secret applies. Local secret coverage means Booster can authenticate a matching delivery; it does not prove that a remote Git provider webhook exists. Configure and verify every remote webhook separately. A webhook you created yourself cannot be validated by Booster; a verified delivery in Activity shows it works."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:461
msgid "A repository webhook set up by Booster has three buttons: Set up (Repair when a recorded hook needs reconfiguring), Validate and Remove. A button that is unavailable stays visible but disabled, with a reason. While an operation runs its button shows a busy state, and the result appears directly under the buttons. Validate checks the settings without changing anything and, only if they are confirmed, requests a ping; the result shows Settings and Ping lines. An accepted ping request is not proof of signed delivery; only a verified signed delivery is. Remove asks for confirmation, deletes the hook at the Git provider and clears Booster’s local recovery record."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:462
msgid "Booster does not record such a webhook, so Set up, Validate and Remove stay unavailable for it and it cannot be repaired or removed from Booster. While Booster holds no webhook record for a repository, it shows Signed delivery received with the time of the last verified signed delivery. That is delivery evidence, not proof that a webhook still exists: a webhook removed through Booster leaves its earlier deliveries in the history."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:463
msgid "Security, delivery history and hosting"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:464
msgid "HMAC verification authorizes deployment after WordPress accepts the request; it is not DDoS protection for the network, web server, PHP workers or WordPress bootstrap. Use public HTTPS with certificate verification enabled, a unique generated secret for each repository, the Git provider’s JSON delivery option and only the required push event. Rotate or disable a suspected secret."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:465
msgid "Do not cache, challenge, redirect or transform either accepted callback form: the pretty /wp-json/ route or the equivalent ?rest_route= query route. Proxies must preserve the raw body and signature headers. WordPress may already have buffered the body before Booster runs, so Core enforces its measured 256 KiB limit but does not trust or parse Content-Length as transport protection."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:466
msgid "A Git provider can time out after Booster has durably admitted work. Git provider delivery history remains authoritative for request duration, response status and redelivery. Compare the Git provider identifier with the Git provider request ID shown on a webhook attempt in Booster Activity. Probes, ignored events and zero-target deliveries may create no attempt, so absence from Activity is inconclusive."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:467
msgid "At a trusted edge or host, you may bound body and header size, read time and connections for both callback forms. IP ranges published by the Git provider are optional defence in depth only where the real peer address is trustworthy; keep them current and retain HMAC. Host, Origin, Referer, forwarded headers, reverse DNS, hidden paths and in-WordPress IP checks do not prove webhook identity."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:468
msgid "Retained webhook setup and cleanup"
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:469
msgid "Switching a package to Published releases does not remove any existing remote webhook or local signing-secret setup. That package ignores pushes while it remains release-managed, while any branch-managed package using the same repository can continue to need the webhook. Keeping the setup is useful for a temporary switch back and forth."
msgstr ""

#: views/documentation.php:470
